  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-copelandi/",
      "html": "<p>This tetra is the most gracile of the so-called \"rosy tetras\". The rosy tetras are a species-rich clade of tetras that share the following characters: a comparatively deep body, a humeral spot (the spot on the shoulder), long fins in males and, in females, an obvious white-black-white marking in the dorsal fin. The phantom tetras, rosy tetras, bleeding hearts and so on belong to that group.</p><p>Fully grown specimens are about 4 cm long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-copelandi/",
      "html": "<p>Hyphessobrycon copelandi is a sought-after species. This can be concluded from the fact that it has a quite well-known common name in German: Federsalmler, which means \"Feather tetra\". Nevertheless it is extremely rare in the trade. Most often, enthusiasts had to pick up single specimens out of importations of cardinal tetras.</p><p>A good number of fully grown specimens (about 4 cm long) have now been imported from Brazil, so perhaps a breeder will now build up an aquarium population of this pretty fish.</p>"
    }
  ],
